---
# The subject of this policy is FLARESOLVERR, not prowlarr — it lives in the prowlarr role
# because flaresolverr has no role of its own, and prowlarr is its only caller. prowlarr's own
# policy is the sibling `networkpolicy-prowlarr.yaml.j2`. The file was called
# `networkpolicy.yaml.j2` until 2026-08-24, where the generic name next to a `-prowlarr` sibling
# read as "prowlarr's policy" and a review took it for one.
#
# Reproduces the `prowlarr-flaresolverr` Docker network, which existed so that a headless browser
# rendering attacker-supplied Cloudflare pages was reachable by prowlarr and nothing else.
# Collapsing the fleet into one namespace would have widened that to every workload in
# {{ k8s_namespace }} — including qbittorrent's untrusted payloads and jellyfin. This makes the
# move a NARROWING instead, which is the same argument n8n-broker's policy makes.
#
# INGRESS, and that word is doing work. Measured on this cluster 2026-08-07 (slice 4, B3): an
# Egress policy selects pods correctly and blocks nothing. Ingress policies ARE enforced —
# n8n-broker relies on one and probes it. So this is written in the direction that holds, and
# netpol-probe-job.yaml.j2 checks it on every deploy rather than trusting the asymmetry.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: flaresolverr
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: flaresolverr
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: prowlarr
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: {{ prowlarr_k8s_flaresolverr_port }}
